1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:06,120
In the previous unit, we ran the analysis
on the HPC and answered three questions:

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:11,181
transplantation sharply reduces microbial
richness; in the longitudinal model,

3
00:00:11,181 --> 00:00:13,776
the auto-FMT group declines more slowly;

4
00:00:13,776 --> 00:00:16,661
and the bacteria that rise and fall over
time

5
00:00:16,661 --> 00:00:19,290
are Enterococcaceae and Lachnospiraceae.

6
00:00:20,233 --> 00:00:25,372
With questions, answers, and data in hand,
writing a paper gets much easier.

7
00:00:25,372 --> 00:00:28,482
But a paper needs two more things: prior
work,

8
00:00:28,482 --> 00:00:32,471
and a traceable source for every number
and every citation.

9
00:00:32,471 --> 00:00:36,798
In Unit 06, we write it up as an English
paper together with AI.

10
00:00:37,733 --> 00:00:41,886
The folder already holds a research brief,
the raw tables of numbers,

11
00:00:41,886 --> 00:00:45,918
and four skills: arxiv for searching,
paper-library for collecting,

12
00:00:45,918 --> 00:00:48,687
research-paper-writing for writing
principles,

13
00:00:48,687 --> 00:00:51,456
and arxiv-preprint for typesetting and
checks.

14
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,946
Before we start, what is the research
brief?

15
00:00:54,946 --> 00:00:59,633
It condenses the previous unit's results
onto one page: three research questions,

16
00:00:59,633 --> 00:01:04,205
their answers, the study limitations, and
the raw table each number comes from.

17
00:01:04,205 --> 00:01:07,735
With it, the AI knows from the start what
the paper is about.

18
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:10,887
The raw tables live in data/tables.

19
00:01:10,887 --> 00:01:15,762
They are what you produced on the HPC: 13
files, covering the filtering steps,

20
00:01:15,762 --> 00:01:19,887
DADA2 denoising stats, Kruskal-Wallis
tests for several groupings,

21
00:01:19,887 --> 00:01:24,637
the longitudinal model coefficients, and
the random forest and PCoA results.

22
00:01:24,637 --> 00:01:27,700
Every number in the paper must be read
from here.

23
00:01:28,633 --> 00:01:29,876
There are five steps.

24
00:01:29,876 --> 00:01:32,836
Read the brief, to align on the paper's
direction.

25
00:01:32,836 --> 00:01:36,626
Find literature, because a paper must say
what others have done,

26
00:01:36,626 --> 00:01:38,579
and the limitations need support.

27
00:01:38,579 --> 00:01:42,368
Take notes and build a comparison table:
copy quotable passages,

28
00:01:42,368 --> 00:01:46,453
and see what others did and what we are
missing. Then write the paper.

29
00:01:46,453 --> 00:01:50,065
Finally, check every citation and number,
and export the PDF.

30
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,610
Step one needs no skill.

31
00:01:52,610 --> 00:01:58,177
We ask the AI to read the research brief,
check the numbers against the raw tables,

32
00:01:58,177 --> 00:02:02,872
and tell us the questions, answers, and
limitations in plain language.

33
00:02:02,872 --> 00:02:05,957
The AI reports that the numbers match
exactly,

34
00:02:05,957 --> 00:02:08,305
and explains six study limitations.

35
00:02:09,267 --> 00:02:11,650
Step two searches with the arxiv skill.

36
00:02:11,650 --> 00:02:15,744
We ask it to find related papers using the
brief's search keywords,

37
00:02:15,744 --> 00:02:20,571
and to list candidates for us to choose
from, without downloading anything yet.

38
00:02:20,571 --> 00:02:24,176
The AI lists the candidates and marks
eight as recommended.

39
00:02:25,133 --> 00:02:29,812
Next, the paper-library skill adds every
recommended arXiv paper:

40
00:02:29,812 --> 00:02:34,852
it downloads the PDFs, renames the files,
and writes the bibliography.

41
00:02:34,852 --> 00:02:40,035
The original clinical trial, QIIME 2,
DADA2, and SILVA are not on arXiv,

42
00:02:40,035 --> 00:02:43,563
so one more sentence registers these four
by DOI.

43
00:02:43,563 --> 00:02:45,866
The library now holds 12 papers.

44
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:50,508
Step three needs no skill; the AI reads
the PDFs directly.

45
00:02:50,508 --> 00:02:55,174
We ask for a note on each paper, with
quotable passages and page numbers.

46
00:02:55,174 --> 00:03:00,544
Then we ask for a literature matrix: what
others have done, and what we are missing.

47
00:03:00,544 --> 00:03:05,210
Each of the six limitations is matched to
a solution from the literature.

48
00:03:06,167 --> 00:03:07,922
Step four is writing. First,

49
00:03:07,922 --> 00:03:12,863
the arxiv-preprint skill sets up an
English paper skeleton in the manuscript

50
00:03:12,863 --> 00:03:14,748
folder, one file per section.

51
00:03:14,748 --> 00:03:17,543
We get a main file and seven section
files,

52
00:03:17,543 --> 00:03:20,144
and an empty two-page skeleton compiles.

53
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:27,240
Then we write one section at a time, with
just one sentence each.

54
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:31,647
The order is Methods, Results,
Introduction, Discussion, Abstract,

55
00:03:31,647 --> 00:03:33,182
and finally, the title.

56
00:03:34,133 --> 00:03:37,300
Sentence one: refer to the
research-paper-writing skill

57
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:39,851
and write the Methods in English.

58
00:03:39,851 --> 00:03:44,335
The AI writes four subsections: data
source and five-stage filtering,

59
00:03:44,335 --> 00:03:48,299
DADA2 denoising and SILVA classification,
diversity analysis,

60
00:03:48,299 --> 00:03:51,223
and the longitudinal model and random
forest.

61
00:03:51,223 --> 00:03:55,316
Software versions and parameters come from
the analysis script.

62
00:03:56,267 --> 00:04:00,200
Sentence two: write the Results,
describing only the data,

63
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:03,116
and redraw any figures from the raw
tables.

64
00:04:03,116 --> 00:04:07,863
We get two tables, for the filtering steps
and the model coefficients,

65
00:04:07,863 --> 00:04:10,711
plus four figures redrawn from the tables.

66
00:04:10,711 --> 00:04:14,441
This section reports numbers only, with no
conclusions.

67
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:18,038
Sentence three: write the Introduction.

68
00:04:18,038 --> 00:04:21,554
This is where most citations go: 9 of the
12 papers.

69
00:04:21,554 --> 00:04:26,153
Take this sentence: single rarefying
discards valid sequencing data,

70
00:04:26,153 --> 00:04:28,994
followed by McMurdie 2013 and Raspet 2024.

71
00:04:28,994 --> 00:04:32,069
That claim traces back to the passage and
page

72
00:04:32,069 --> 00:04:34,877
number copied word for word in the notes.

73
00:04:34,877 --> 00:04:39,273
That's how an introduction works: a line
of background, a source,

74
00:04:39,273 --> 00:04:42,046
and finally the three research questions.

75
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,969
Sentence four: write the Discussion based
on the literature matrix,

76
00:04:46,969 --> 00:04:49,219
including limitations and conclusions.

77
00:04:49,219 --> 00:04:51,115
It opens with the main findings,

78
00:04:51,115 --> 00:04:54,550
compares them with the original trial and
earlier methods,

79
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:57,808
then lists the six limitations and how to
address them,

80
00:04:57,808 --> 00:05:02,606
such as the unconverged model and single
rarefying, and ends with the conclusion.

81
00:05:03,567 --> 00:05:06,595
Sentence five: write a structured
abstract.

82
00:05:06,595 --> 00:05:12,018
Only after the full text is done is it
condensed into four parts: background,

83
00:05:12,018 --> 00:05:14,413
methods, results, and conclusions.

84
00:05:14,413 --> 00:05:18,427
It comes to 1,910 characters, just under
the arXiv limit.

85
00:05:19,367 --> 00:05:24,143
Sentence six: propose three candidate
titles and say which one you recommend.

86
00:05:24,143 --> 00:05:25,879
The AI recommends the first,

87
00:05:25,879 --> 00:05:30,655
framing the work as a methodological case
study of a longitudinal reanalysis.

88
00:05:30,655 --> 00:05:34,004
Then: use candidate title 1, and add the
declarations,

89
00:05:34,004 --> 00:05:38,407
leaving author contributions, conflicts of
interest, and funding blank.

90
00:05:39,367 --> 00:05:40,787
Step five is checking.

91
00:05:40,787 --> 00:05:44,787
First, the arxiv-preprint skill checks and
compiles the paper:

92
00:05:44,787 --> 00:05:48,723
a script checks citations, figure files,
and abstract length.

93
00:05:48,723 --> 00:05:52,918
Then we ask the AI to verify every
citation sentence by sentence,

94
00:05:52,918 --> 00:05:55,886
and trace every number back to the raw
tables.

95
00:05:55,886 --> 00:05:59,887
It caught two citations that claimed too
much, and fixed them.

96
00:06:00,833 --> 00:06:06,027
The last sentence compiles directly with
the local pdflatex and bibtex.

97
00:06:06,027 --> 00:06:10,637
The result is a 12-page English paper in
arXiv preprint format,

98
00:06:10,637 --> 00:06:15,319
citing 12 references, with no errors and
no undefined citations.

99
00:06:15,319 --> 00:06:19,708
Without LaTeX, you can upload to Overleaf
and compile there.

100
00:06:20,667 --> 00:06:24,876
Looking back, the whole paper used four
skills: arxiv to search,

101
00:06:24,876 --> 00:06:28,690
paper-library to collect,
research-paper-writing to write,

102
00:06:28,690 --> 00:06:31,320
and arxiv-preprint to typeset and check.

103
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:36,384
Every other step was just telling the AI,
in plain language, what to do next.

104
00:06:37,333 --> 00:06:39,821
This is a practice draft. Before
submitting,

105
00:06:39,821 --> 00:06:43,291
the authors must personally check the
numbers and citations,

106
00:06:43,291 --> 00:06:45,200
and confirm the ethics statement.

107
00:06:46,167 --> 00:06:48,208
Unit 06, Research Paper Workflow.

108
00:06:48,208 --> 00:06:51,115
Open the README, start with the research
brief,

109
00:06:51,115 --> 00:06:53,527
and try it with your own research data.

